WEBVTT

00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:05.125
Welcome to Day 3: Time Management, Calendar and Travel.

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:07.862
We start with time management and productivity,

00:00:07.862 --> 00:00:11.150
move on to calendar management, then legal calendaring,

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.400
and finish with travel management.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.375
Forty-four lessons in all.

00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:20.900
By the end of today, you'll be able to prioritize and protect the executive's time,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.712
keep a calendar that actually holds under pressure,

00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:28.025
track court deadlines precisely, and plan travel end-to-end.

00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:33.788
Section 1 of 4: Time Management and Productivity.

00:00:33.788 --> 00:00:38.812
Eighteen lessons, from prioritization frameworks to energy management and handling

00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:40.100
interruptions.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.025
When everything feels important, you need a way to decide what actually gets your

00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:47.000
time.

00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:52.125
Think of these as four different lenses, each fixing a different problem.

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:57.550
The four-box grid is the one you'll use most: do now, schedule, hand off and let go.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.625
First, when the list is so long you don't know where to start,

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:04.750
sort it into those four boxes.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.250
You'll find a lot of it doesn't need you at all.

00:01:08.075 --> 00:01:11.788
Next, once you know what matters, use short bursts of focus:

00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:15.175
about 25 minutes on one thing, then a short break.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:21.025
Then, give the most important work its own spot on the calendar, away from email.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.438
After that, every so often, ask which small share of your work is producing most of

00:01:27.438 --> 00:01:30.150
the results, and give it more of your time.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.887
Finally, don't try all four at once.

00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:35.775
Pick the one that fixes your biggest problem this week,

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:38.250
get good at it, then add another.

00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:41.312
Here's a situation to work through.

00:01:41.312 --> 00:01:46.700
Elias says he spends four hours a day in email and still misses important things.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.125
Using one of the four frameworks, how would you restructure his inbox day?

00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:53.962
Quick check.

00:01:53.962 --> 00:01:58.625
Which prioritization technique focuses on the 20 percent of tasks that produce 80

00:01:58.625 --> 00:02:00.175
percent of results?

00:02:00.175 --> 00:02:03.912
Is it the 80/20 Rule, also called the Pareto Principle,

00:02:03.912 --> 00:02:08.112
time blocking, the Eisenhower Matrix, or the Pomodoro Technique?

00:02:08.112 --> 00:02:09.425
Take a second.

00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:11.987
It's the 80/20 Rule.

00:02:11.987 --> 00:02:15.612
Pareto's principle is about impact concentration:

00:02:15.612 --> 00:02:19.000
a small slice of effort driving most of the outcome.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.788
When two truly important things arrive at once,

00:02:23.788 --> 00:02:27.050
ask what actually breaks if each one waits ten minutes.

00:02:27.825 --> 00:02:33.062
A collision isn't normal prioritizing, where one thing is obviously more urgent.

00:02:33.062 --> 00:02:36.525
Both matter, and neither can simply be postponed.

00:02:37.325 --> 00:02:40.400
First, weigh the real cost of delay on each side.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.038
Next, where you can, do a little on both: a quick acknowledgment on one while you

00:02:45.038 --> 00:02:46.675
fully handle the other.

00:02:46.675 --> 00:02:50.237
Finally, if it's genuinely too close to call, ask.

00:02:50.237 --> 00:02:53.175
A 30-second check-in beats a wrong guess.

00:02:53.925 --> 00:02:55.638
Take a second with this one.

00:02:55.638 --> 00:03:00.450
In the same minute, Elias asks you to get opposing counsel on the phone right now,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.750
and a major client emails that their wire transfer failed and the closing is at

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.662
noon.

00:03:05.662 --> 00:03:07.800
What happens first, and why?

00:03:09.775 --> 00:03:12.825
Knowing what matters isn't enough; you have to decide it,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.900
put it on the calendar, guard it and check it held.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.312
Most people stop after the first step.

00:03:18.312 --> 00:03:22.900
That's why this is four steps, in order, and skipping one breaks the chain.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.900
First, decide.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.488
Before you open the calendar, ask what really deserves protected time this week.

00:03:29.488 --> 00:03:32.125
That's a judgment call, not a scheduling one.

00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:36.225
Next, block it, early, before other people's requests fill the gaps.

00:03:36.225 --> 00:03:40.288
Then, protect it, like any other meeting you've already committed to.

00:03:40.288 --> 00:03:41.975
Finally, review it.

00:03:41.975 --> 00:03:46.075
Did the protected time actually happen, or did something 'urgent' take it every

00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:47.025
time?

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.138
Here's a situation to work through.

00:03:50.138 --> 00:03:54.712
Elias says his priority this quarter is winning the Meridian arbitration.

00:03:54.712 --> 00:03:58.175
His last four weeks show only three hours spent on it.

00:03:58.175 --> 00:04:03.200
Is this a time management problem, a calendar management problem, or both?

00:04:05.125 --> 00:04:07.800
We don't just manage the executive's calendar.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.675
We design it, the way an investor decides where money goes.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.913
Think of his time as a portfolio.

00:04:14.913 --> 00:04:19.213
Some goes to work that brings in revenue, some to long-term growth,

00:04:19.213 --> 00:04:23.912
some to legal and compliance deadlines, some to events where his reputation is on

00:04:23.912 --> 00:04:28.300
the line, and some to personal commitments that genuinely matter.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.075
So first, before we defend a block of time, we name what it's for.

00:04:33.075 --> 00:04:35.425
If we can't, it isn't protected.

00:04:35.425 --> 00:04:38.250
It's just empty space waiting to be filled.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.600
Finally, we treat the calendar as an ongoing decision,

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.675
and regularly check it still matches what matters.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:48.562
Think about this.

00:04:48.562 --> 00:04:53.938
Every week, Elias's Friday afternoon 'strategy time' gets taken by last-minute

00:04:53.938 --> 00:04:55.037
requests.

00:04:55.037 --> 00:04:58.000
What would investing that time on purpose look like?

00:04:59.975 --> 00:05:02.588
No conflicts doesn't mean no problems.

00:05:02.588 --> 00:05:06.662
Picture a day that's back-to-back reactive meetings from nine to five.

00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:07.950
Technically clean.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.875
But when does the real work happen?

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:14.725
The question isn't just 'are there any clashes?' It's 'is this week actually set up

00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:16.300
for the things that matter?'

00:05:17.125 --> 00:05:19.500
First, check for meeting overload.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.675
Next, confirm that protected space exists for this week's most important work.

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:30.662
Then, apply the same thinking to travel weeks: the days before,

00:05:30.662 --> 00:05:33.125
during and after a trip need care too.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.075
After that, when a week goes wrong, work out why.

00:05:38.075 --> 00:05:42.325
Did we choose the wrong priority, or the right one and fail to protect it?

00:05:42.325 --> 00:05:46.825
Finally, do this check every week, not only when something falls apart.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:48.762
Try this one.

00:05:48.762 --> 00:05:51.725
Elias is in London Tuesday to Thursday.

00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:55.050
Monday, the day before he flies, has seven meetings;

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.062
Friday, the day after he lands, has six.

00:05:58.062 --> 00:06:00.850
How would you fix the days either side of the trip?

00:06:02.775 --> 00:06:07.000
An open slot at someone's worst hour of the day isn't really open.

00:06:07.775 --> 00:06:10.562
Time management asks when something should happen.

00:06:10.562 --> 00:06:15.000
Energy management asks whether this person will be at their best at that moment.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.300
Most of us are sharp in the morning, strong until lunch,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.438
slump mid-afternoon, then get a second wind late in the day.

00:06:22.438 --> 00:06:24.362
That's the curve on the slide.

00:06:24.362 --> 00:06:28.950
Book a tough negotiation into the slump, and you've scheduled it for failure.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.425
First, learn the executive's pattern.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.750
Next, save the sharp hours for work that needs a clear head,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.375
like big decisions and negotiations.

00:06:41.175 --> 00:06:45.925
Then, check anything high-stakes against their low times before you confirm it.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.000
After that, if it has to go in a low time, say so.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.025
Flag it as a risk, rather than pretending the slot is neutral.

00:06:55.025 --> 00:06:58.125
Finally, check the pattern again now and then.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.738
Here's a scheduling puzzle.

00:07:00.738 --> 00:07:06.213
Elias is sharpest from 8 to 11 a.m. and fades after 3 p.m. This week you have a

00:07:06.213 --> 00:07:11.050
tough negotiation, a routine staff check-in, expense sign-offs and a strategy

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.063
session.

00:07:12.063 --> 00:07:13.625
Where does each one go?

00:07:15.575 --> 00:07:20.225
A free slot on the calendar isn't the same as having the energy to use it.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.313
An exhausted executive makes expensive mistakes.

00:07:24.313 --> 00:07:29.500
After a full day of mediation, an open hour at 6 p.m. has nothing left in it for a

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.975
demanding call.

00:07:30.975 --> 00:07:36.175
So we avoid stacking draining meetings, protect focus time and filter out low-value

00:07:36.175 --> 00:07:37.350
requests.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:43.075
First, we build recovery breaks after the genuinely demanding events on purpose.

00:07:43.075 --> 00:07:47.475
Finally, we notice patterns, like a recurring meeting that always leaves him

00:07:47.475 --> 00:07:51.525
drained, and we raise it instead of silently putting up with it.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.400
Here's a situation to work through.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.575
You support Elias at work and his family at home.

00:07:57.575 --> 00:08:03.363
His Saturday has a work call at 9, a school event at 11 and a dinner party at 7.

00:08:03.363 --> 00:08:06.975
Where would you build in recovery time, and what might you move?

00:08:08.925 --> 00:08:12.950
One small interruption can eat your whole afternoon if you let it.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.675
Someone leans over your desk with a 'quick question'.

00:08:16.675 --> 00:08:18.688
The question takes two minutes.

00:08:18.688 --> 00:08:22.188
The damage is the twenty minutes it takes to find your place again.

00:08:22.188 --> 00:08:24.875
In this job you can't avoid interruptions.

00:08:24.875 --> 00:08:28.800
The skill is handling them in seconds, so they stay small.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.175
First, decide fast whether it's truly urgent, or just feels urgent because someone

00:08:34.175 --> 00:08:35.900
is standing in front of you.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.875
Next, if it can wait, write it down somewhere you'll see later.

00:08:39.875 --> 00:08:44.025
Then, go back to exactly where you were and finish that thought.

00:08:44.025 --> 00:08:48.375
Finally, handle the things that can wait together later, in one go.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.225
Try the four moves on this one.

00:08:51.225 --> 00:08:55.338
You're proofreading a court filing due at 4 p.m. At 3:15,

00:08:55.338 --> 00:08:59.550
a partner asks you to 'quickly' reformat a slide deck for tomorrow.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:02.450
What do you say, and what do you do with the request?

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:08.200
Before you switch to a new request, leave yourself a clear marker of where you

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.175
stopped.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:14.700
A task injection is a new, unrelated request landing while you're halfway through

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:15.963
something else.

00:09:15.963 --> 00:09:19.913
Unlike a collision, the first task can usually continue.

00:09:19.913 --> 00:09:24.225
The danger is coming back and not remembering exactly where you were.

00:09:25.025 --> 00:09:28.200
First, decide: does the new request need action now,

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.725
or can it wait for a safe stopping point?

00:09:30.725 --> 00:09:35.775
Next, leave that marker: a note, a highlighted line or a saved draft.

00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:38.512
Finally, if the new task takes over completely,

00:09:38.512 --> 00:09:40.975
tell whoever is waiting on the first one.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:43.338
Here's one to think about.

00:09:43.338 --> 00:09:48.300
You were interrupted while updating a client's contact details and forgot to finish.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.000
What habit fixes this?

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:55.375
If it takes less time to do than to write down, just do it.

00:09:56.175 --> 00:10:00.950
Writing a tiny task down, remembering it, finding it later and coming back to it

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.350
takes more effort than simply doing it.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:07.575
First, be honest about the estimate.

00:10:07.575 --> 00:10:11.250
Will it really take under two minutes, or are you hoping it will?

00:10:12.075 --> 00:10:14.900
Next, if it really is that quick, do it now.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:21.125
Then, if it starts growing once you begin, stop and give it a proper slot on the

00:10:21.125 --> 00:10:22.225
calendar.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.775
After that, keep applying the rule all day.

00:10:26.775 --> 00:10:30.838
Finally, look for tiny tasks that have sat on your list for days.

00:10:30.838 --> 00:10:33.075
That's the sign the habit has slipped.

00:10:33.825 --> 00:10:36.088
Pause the video and sort these.

00:10:36.088 --> 00:10:40.150
Confirm a lunch booking, reply 'received' to a court notice,

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:44.975
reformat a 20-page exhibit list, forward an invoice to billing and update a phone

00:10:44.975 --> 00:10:45.938
number.

00:10:45.938 --> 00:10:48.275
Which ones pass the two-minute rule?

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:55.400
Your brain works faster when it does the same kind of task several times in a row.

00:10:56.175 --> 00:11:00.325
You wouldn't chop one onion, wash the board, then come back later for the next

00:11:00.325 --> 00:11:01.288
onion.

00:11:01.288 --> 00:11:06.200
Every jump from a call to an email to a spreadsheet makes your brain warm up again.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:10.000
Batching means all your calls together, then all your replies,

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.025
so you warm up once for each.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.325
First, spot the things that come up again and again: calls, replies, data entry.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.925
Next, give each type its own block of time.

00:11:23.725 --> 00:11:27.850
Then, let non-urgent things wait a little so they can join their batch.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:33.438
After that, if something truly urgent arrives, break the batch.

00:11:33.438 --> 00:11:35.775
Batching is a tool, not a rule.

00:11:35.775 --> 00:11:39.225
Finally, revisit your batches when your workload changes.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.488
Here's a quick test.

00:11:41.488 --> 00:11:45.188
You batch your scheduling emails for 3 p.m. At 10 a.m.,

00:11:45.188 --> 00:11:48.400
a client needs to move today's 1 p.m. meeting.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:49.950
Does it wait for the batch?

00:11:51.875 --> 00:11:56.250
It takes most people over twenty minutes to get fully back into deep work after a

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.725
real interruption.

00:11:58.475 --> 00:12:01.275
And it isn't only other people's interruptions.

00:12:01.275 --> 00:12:03.800
The switches we choose cost us too.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.150
Jumping from a filing to an email to a travel booking feels productive.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:13.375
But every jump means warming up again from cold, and those minutes quietly add up.

00:12:15.325 --> 00:12:20.750
First, use batching and focus blocks so you pay that warm-up cost fewer times a day.

00:12:21.575 --> 00:12:25.375
Next, don't try to do two genuinely different tasks at once.

00:12:25.375 --> 00:12:28.725
It feels efficient and is almost always slower.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:34.912
After that, when you do switch, close off the first task properly before starting

00:12:34.912 --> 00:12:35.975
the next.

00:12:35.975 --> 00:12:38.963
Finally, try counting your switches for one day.

00:12:38.963 --> 00:12:41.225
Almost everyone is surprised.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.363
Think about this hour.

00:12:43.363 --> 00:12:47.750
You touch a legal filing question, a personal travel request for Elias,

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.613
a board deck edit and two Slack pings.

00:12:50.613 --> 00:12:53.275
How would you rearrange it to cut the switching?

00:12:55.175 --> 00:12:57.812
When you switch between business and personal work,

00:12:57.812 --> 00:13:01.525
switch your tone on purpose, or the wrong one will slip through.

00:13:02.325 --> 00:13:07.250
In a combined EA and PA role, you might write a formal client email and answer a

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:09.650
family text two minutes later.

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:13.562
Switch too fast, and a stiff tone lands in the family message,

00:13:13.562 --> 00:13:16.275
or a casual one lands with a client.

00:13:17.075 --> 00:13:20.762
First, before you answer anything, name which world it belongs to:

00:13:20.762 --> 00:13:22.600
business or personal.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.750
Next, finish one fully before you open the other.

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.800
Finally, keep separate task lists for business and personal.

00:13:30.575 --> 00:13:31.950
Here's what that looks like.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:36.562
You text Sarah: 'Per our discussion, please find below the options for Saturday.'

00:13:36.562 --> 00:13:38.350
She replies, 'Are you OK?

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:42.300
You sound like a lawyer.' What slipped, and how do you avoid it?

00:13:44.225 --> 00:13:48.850
Fifteen minutes of planning once a week saves you from rebuilding your priorities

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:50.550
every Monday morning.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:55.638
Some things need several days of preparation, and you only see them if you look at

00:13:55.638 --> 00:13:57.300
the whole week at once.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:02.225
First, pick a fixed time each week and protect it like any meeting.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:06.950
Then, look back at what didn't get done last week,

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:10.675
and decide on purpose whether it still matters or can be dropped.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:17.312
After that, if something needs prep several days ahead, block that prep time now.

00:14:17.312 --> 00:14:19.425
Finally, defend the session.

00:14:19.425 --> 00:14:22.725
If it keeps getting bumped, it isn't a ritual anymore.

00:14:23.475 --> 00:14:25.800
Pause the video and plan backwards.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.750
It's Friday afternoon.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:31.512
Next Thursday, Elias has a mediation that needs a binder,

00:14:31.512 --> 00:14:34.088
two witness calls and a travel booking.

00:14:34.088 --> 00:14:36.075
What goes on the calendar today?

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.950
A quick, honest no with an alternative protects a relationship far better than a yes

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:44.200
you can't keep.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:48.275
We often say yes because no feels uncomfortable.

00:14:48.275 --> 00:14:52.438
But what damages trust is silence, a flat no with no reason,

00:14:52.438 --> 00:14:55.125
or a yes that quietly never gets done.

00:14:57.075 --> 00:14:58.537
First, answer quickly.

00:14:58.537 --> 00:15:00.925
A fast no is kinder than silence.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:05.600
Next, give the real reason, specifically, so it sounds like an answer,

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:06.975
not a brush-off.

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:12.037
Then, offer something: a different time, a different person or part of what they

00:15:12.037 --> 00:15:13.175
asked for.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.250
Finally, if you're genuinely full, say so up front.

00:15:19.025 --> 00:15:19.988
Try it now.

00:15:19.988 --> 00:15:25.075
A partner wants 30 minutes with Elias tomorrow, but his whole day is trial prep.

00:15:25.075 --> 00:15:28.825
Say the no out loud, with the reason and an alternative.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:35.775
A deadline nobody checked against the actual work isn't a deadline; it's a hope.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:40.650
First, think about the actual work involved before you name a date.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:45.238
Next, as an EA you often set dates for work someone else is doing,

00:15:45.238 --> 00:15:49.225
so check with that person before you promise anything on their behalf.

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:54.200
Then, add some breathing room for real uncertainty,

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.500
but don't pad everything by habit.

00:15:58.425 --> 00:16:01.362
After that, say the date clearly with a name attached,

00:16:01.362 --> 00:16:04.300
just like the timeline in an ACT email.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:09.150
Finally, if it starts to slip, say so early, while there are still options.

00:16:09.925 --> 00:16:12.212
Here's a situation to work through.

00:16:12.212 --> 00:16:16.725
Elias promised a client a memo by Monday without asking the associate,

00:16:16.725 --> 00:16:18.675
who's on leave until Tuesday.

00:16:18.675 --> 00:16:19.750
It's Friday.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:20.950
What do you do now?

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:27.175
Record your time as you go, because by Friday your memory is guessing.

00:16:27.925 --> 00:16:32.162
Try remembering exactly what you did last Tuesday at 2 PM.

00:16:32.162 --> 00:16:33.287
You can't.

00:16:33.287 --> 00:16:37.100
That's why time logged at the end of the week comes out vague and short,

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:39.400
and the firm loses money it earned.

00:16:41.375 --> 00:16:45.825
First, describe each entry clearly enough that someone else could understand it.

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:49.025
Next, log the small stuff too.

00:16:49.025 --> 00:16:52.575
Five minutes here and there adds up to real money over a year.

00:16:54.525 --> 00:16:56.750
Then, include calls and emails.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.950
They're real work and the most forgotten.

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:03.600
Finally, keep a weekly summary, so you can see where your time really goes.

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:10.750
Pause the video and rewrite these entries: 'Calls 0.5,' 'Admin 1.2,' 'Harlow stuff

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:16.325
0.8.' Use action, object and purpose, and tag each one to a matter.

00:17:18.225 --> 00:17:22.975
Where you think your time goes and where it actually goes are almost never the same.

00:17:23.775 --> 00:17:27.188
Most of us believe we spend our days on the important work.

00:17:27.188 --> 00:17:31.250
Track a real week, and you'll usually find that work squeezed into the gaps,

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.663
with reactive meetings taking over.

00:17:33.663 --> 00:17:37.125
The gap between those two pictures is the useful part.

00:17:39.075 --> 00:17:42.300
First, track one normal week as it really happens.

00:17:43.125 --> 00:17:46.525
Next, compare it with what you thought your priorities were.

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:51.725
Then, treat this as an occasional check-up, not something you do forever.

00:17:53.675 --> 00:17:56.600
After that, make sure your time tracking is accurate.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.350
Finally, change something next week based on what you found.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:05.800
Your audit shows 40 percent of your week went to rescheduling meetings and only 10

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:09.012
percent to Elias's most important matter.

00:18:09.012 --> 00:18:11.400
What two things do you change next week?

00:18:13.325 --> 00:18:16.538
Section 2 of 4: Calendar Management.

00:18:16.538 --> 00:18:19.275
Eight lessons on keeping a calendar that holds,

00:18:19.275 --> 00:18:24.075
from conflicts and deep-work blocks to time zones and last-minute changes.

00:18:26.025 --> 00:18:30.925
One calendar everyone trusts, with breathing room built in and a quick weekly check,

00:18:30.925 --> 00:18:32.900
is what makes a schedule hold.

00:18:33.675 --> 00:18:36.838
The moment someone keeps a second, private calendar,

00:18:36.838 --> 00:18:39.800
conflicts start hiding in the gap between the two.

00:18:41.775 --> 00:18:46.850
First, put everything in one synced calendar and retire any side calendars.

00:18:47.675 --> 00:18:50.875
Next, leave gaps between commitments by default,

00:18:50.875 --> 00:18:53.975
and let booking tools handle routine scheduling.

00:18:55.900 --> 00:19:00.925
Then, if the firm uses a CRM, make sure scheduling information lives there too,

00:19:00.925 --> 00:19:02.200
not scattered.

00:19:04.125 --> 00:19:07.875
After that, treat deep-work time as seriously as a meeting.

00:19:07.875 --> 00:19:12.700
Finally, review the calendar once a week to catch the slow creep of conflicts.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:14.613
Picture this.

00:19:14.613 --> 00:19:18.300
Elias has a firm calendar, a personal Google calendar,

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:23.875
and a paper diary his old assistant used for 'private things.' Last week a client

00:19:23.875 --> 00:19:26.562
meeting landed on top of a doctor's appointment.

00:19:26.562 --> 00:19:29.375
How do you make one calendar everyone trusts?

00:19:31.275 --> 00:19:35.050
When two meetings collide, you flag it straight away and recommend,

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:37.100
but the executive decides.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:40.425
It's tempting to just fix it quietly.

00:19:40.425 --> 00:19:43.450
But Elias might know something the calendar doesn't show,

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:47.050
like a delicate conversation or a big opportunity.

00:19:47.825 --> 00:19:50.362
First, tell him as soon as you spot the clash.

00:19:50.362 --> 00:19:52.950
Don't rebook or decline anything on your own.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:57.875
Next, weigh each meeting by how much it matters, not by which was booked first.

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:03.000
Then, lay out the trade-off and say which way you'd go.

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:07.225
After that, write down what was decided.

00:20:07.225 --> 00:20:10.625
Finally, let both sides know the outcome quickly.

00:20:11.375 --> 00:20:12.700
Try this one.

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:17.450
Thursday at 11 a.m., a partner has booked Elias for a budget review,

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:21.262
and a major client has just asked for a call at the same time.

00:20:21.262 --> 00:20:24.075
You could easily move the budget review yourself.

00:20:24.075 --> 00:20:27.850
What do you do instead, and what does your message to Elias say?

00:20:29.775 --> 00:20:33.550
A focus block anyone can book over is just a suggestion.

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:37.525
If a calendar only shows meetings, the thinking work,

00:20:37.525 --> 00:20:40.650
the drafting and the reviewing, has nowhere to live,

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:42.938
so it gets squeezed into evenings.

00:20:42.938 --> 00:20:47.300
A real block is one that can't be taken without a conversation first.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:53.875
First, put real, uninterrupted time on the calendar for focused work.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:59.425
Next, use it for the most important work, not whatever's easiest to fit.

00:21:01.350 --> 00:21:06.175
After that, if it does get double-booked, treat that as a real conflict to sort out.

00:21:06.175 --> 00:21:09.113
Finally, check whether the blocks are being used.

00:21:09.113 --> 00:21:12.625
One that's always skipped needs fixing or rethinking.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.638
Here's a situation to think about.

00:21:15.638 --> 00:21:21.000
Elias has a protected block every Tuesday afternoon, but he uses it for email.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.663
Meanwhile, the Meridian brief is always done late at night.

00:21:24.663 --> 00:21:27.325
How do you raise it, and what would you suggest?

00:21:29.275 --> 00:21:33.950
Five to ten minutes between meetings is part of the job, not dead space.

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:38.788
Back-to-back, every meeting either starts late or ends in a rush.

00:21:38.788 --> 00:21:43.600
A short gap gives time to jot down what just happened and get ready for what's next.

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:49.250
First, make a short gap between meetings your normal setting.

00:21:50.075 --> 00:21:53.788
Next, for Elias specifically, remember his standing rule:

00:21:53.788 --> 00:21:56.375
a debrief buffer after key sessions.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:02.575
After that, if a day is too packed for gaps, say so instead of silently stacking

00:22:02.575 --> 00:22:03.650
meetings.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:08.200
Finally, check each week, because back-to-back booking sneaks back in.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.638
Take a second with this one.

00:22:10.638 --> 00:22:14.363
A new partner books Elias for a call right after a court hearing,

00:22:14.363 --> 00:22:16.500
not knowing about the debrief rule.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:20.200
What do you say to the partner, and how do you stop it happening again?

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:26.450
If nobody can say what a meeting is for, it's a good candidate to cut.

00:22:27.225 --> 00:22:31.725
Recurring meetings are easy to start, and almost nobody ever ends them.

00:22:31.725 --> 00:22:35.450
Months later, the reason is gone, but the meeting isn't.

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:40.600
First, every quarter or so, ask of each one: does this still need to happen,

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.725
this often, with all these people?

00:22:42.725 --> 00:22:47.025
Next, try the one-sentence test: can anyone say what it's for?

00:22:48.975 --> 00:22:52.250
Then, point out meetings that have outlived their purpose.

00:22:54.175 --> 00:22:57.300
After that, suggest something specific: cancel it,

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.625
shorten it or invite fewer people.

00:22:59.625 --> 00:23:02.700
Finally, check back later that the change stuck.

00:23:03.475 --> 00:23:04.813
Think about this one.

00:23:04.813 --> 00:23:08.688
Elias has a weekly 60-minute 'matter sync' with eight people,

00:23:08.688 --> 00:23:11.550
no agenda and half of them with cameras off.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.000
What would you suggest?

00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:19.125
A time that's easy for you might be the middle of the night for someone else.

00:23:19.875 --> 00:23:24.625
When people are spread across cities, check everyone's local time, every time.

00:23:24.625 --> 00:23:29.200
And watch daylight saving, because different countries switch on different dates.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:36.000
First, check the local time for every person on the invite, not just your own.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:39.700
Next, name the time zone in the invite itself.

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:46.225
Then, double-check repeating meetings around the daylight saving changes.

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:51.550
After that, choose a time that's fair to the person worst off,

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:53.700
not just convenient for most.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:58.775
Finally, if a mix-up happens, fix it right away and confirm with everyone.

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:00.613
Try this.

00:24:00.613 --> 00:24:05.825
Elias needs a call with co-counsel in London and a client in Manila this week.

00:24:05.825 --> 00:24:09.950
Find a time that works for all three, and write the line in the invite that names

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:11.325
the time zone.

00:24:13.225 --> 00:24:17.325
Before you say yes to a last-minute change, look at what it knocks over.

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:21.150
Moving one meeting is rarely just one meeting.

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:25.675
It can push into the next one, cut into prep time or clash with travel.

00:24:26.475 --> 00:24:29.800
So first, when a change arrives, don't confirm it yet.

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:32.450
Next, see what it pushes out of the way.

00:24:34.375 --> 00:24:36.750
Then, work out who else is affected.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:41.588
After that, tell everyone involved clearly.

00:24:41.588 --> 00:24:44.250
Quietly editing the calendar isn't enough.

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:48.425
Finally, recheck the rest of the day, and if the same person keeps causing

00:24:48.425 --> 00:24:51.700
last-minute changes, deal with that separately.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:58.088
At 10:15, a client asks to move their 2 p.m. meeting to 11:30 today.

00:24:58.088 --> 00:25:03.025
That slot has Elias's debrief buffer and a call with a junior associate.

00:25:03.025 --> 00:25:06.175
What do you check before you say yes, and who do you tell?

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:12.800
Check every calendar before you confirm anything, not just the one in front of you.

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:18.988
Many executives live on several calendars: work, personal, board roles.

00:25:18.988 --> 00:25:23.363
A clash can hide in plain sight because each one looks clear on its own.

00:25:23.363 --> 00:25:27.400
The master view on the slide is simply the habit of looking across all of them at

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:28.375
once.

00:25:30.325 --> 00:25:34.450
First, find out every calendar the executive actually uses.

00:25:35.275 --> 00:25:38.100
Next, check any new booking against all of them.

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:42.475
Then, build a single overall view.

00:25:44.425 --> 00:25:48.575
After that, keep work and personal calendars separate for privacy.

00:25:48.575 --> 00:25:53.075
Finally, raise a clash across calendars like any other conflict.

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:54.813
Try this.

00:25:54.813 --> 00:25:58.300
A partner asks you to book Elias for Saturday morning.

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:03.213
His firm calendar is empty, but the family calendar shows a school event.

00:26:03.213 --> 00:26:07.425
What do you do, without sharing private family details with the partner?

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.538
Section 3 of 4: Legal Calendaring.

00:26:12.538 --> 00:26:15.625
Seven lessons on court deadlines and trial work,

00:26:15.625 --> 00:26:19.850
including court docketing, counting legal deadlines and e-filing.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:25.575
With court deadlines, one missed reminder should never be enough to miss the

00:26:25.575 --> 00:26:26.650
deadline.

00:26:27.425 --> 00:26:31.875
Missing one can cost a client their rights or expose the firm to a malpractice

00:26:31.875 --> 00:26:32.763
claim.

00:26:32.763 --> 00:26:35.950
So a good docketing system has backups on purpose.

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:39.638
First, enter each deadline as soon as you know it,

00:26:39.638 --> 00:26:42.875
straight from the court order or filing confirmation.

00:26:42.875 --> 00:26:47.213
Next, set reminders at several points, for example two weeks out,

00:26:47.213 --> 00:26:49.350
three days out and on the day.

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:54.625
Finally, regularly compare the docket with the case file,

00:26:54.625 --> 00:26:58.550
and close off deadlines that are done so the open ones stand out.

00:26:59.325 --> 00:27:01.388
Here's a situation to think about.

00:27:01.388 --> 00:27:05.838
An associate emails: 'The judge gave us until the 15th for the reply,

00:27:05.838 --> 00:27:09.550
please calendar it.' The order itself isn't attached.

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:11.775
What do you do before you enter the date?

00:27:13.675 --> 00:27:17.375
A court deadline missed by one day can cost a client their case,

00:27:17.375 --> 00:27:19.750
so the counting itself is a skill.

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:25.888
In federal court, you count every calendar day, weekends and holidays included.

00:27:25.888 --> 00:27:28.563
You don't count the day of the event that starts the clock,

00:27:28.563 --> 00:27:33.388
you do count the last day, and if the last day falls on a weekend or holiday,

00:27:33.388 --> 00:27:35.700
it moves to the next business day.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:40.525
Many state courts count 'court days' instead, which skip weekends and court

00:27:40.525 --> 00:27:41.625
holidays.

00:27:41.625 --> 00:27:45.238
And how a paper was served can add time; in federal court,

00:27:45.238 --> 00:27:47.775
service by mail adds three days.

00:27:49.725 --> 00:27:52.950
First, we find the trigger, the event that starts the clock,

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:54.600
and note where it came from.

00:27:55.425 --> 00:28:00.200
Next, we find the rule that sets the period, and whether it's calendar days or court

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:02.700
days, including local rules.

00:28:03.475 --> 00:28:06.375
Then we count, and check where the last day lands.

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:12.838
After that, we enter it in the docketing calendar with the rule cited and reminders

00:28:12.838 --> 00:28:15.725
at 14, 7 and 2 days.

00:28:15.725 --> 00:28:20.525
Finally, a second person confirms every calculated court deadline.

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:22.788
Now try a count.

00:28:22.788 --> 00:28:26.450
A motion is served electronically on Friday, October 2,

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:30.513
in federal court, and the response is due in 14 days.

00:28:30.513 --> 00:28:31.688
When is it due?

00:28:31.688 --> 00:28:35.025
What changes if the court counts court days instead?

00:28:36.925 --> 00:28:37.650
Quick check.

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:42.013
You're counting a 10-day response deadline from the day a motion was served.

00:28:42.013 --> 00:28:43.750
Which day is day 1?

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.575
Is it the day the motion was served, the day after the motion was served,

00:28:47.575 --> 00:28:51.950
the first business day of the following week, or whichever day the attorney prefers

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:52.938
to use?

00:28:52.938 --> 00:28:54.250
Take a second.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:56.388
It's the day after.

00:28:56.388 --> 00:29:01.538
The day of the event that starts the clock isn't counted, so day 1 is the next day.

00:29:01.538 --> 00:29:06.125
Then check the court's rules and whether the last day lands on a weekend or court

00:29:06.125 --> 00:29:07.175
holiday.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:12.150
A brief that's written perfectly but filed wrong is,

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.150
as far as the court's concerned, not filed.

00:29:15.925 --> 00:29:19.000
Two words to keep apart: filing and service.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.225
Filing gives the document to the court.

00:29:21.225 --> 00:29:23.550
Service delivers it to the other side.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.325
Federal courts use a system called CM/ECF for e-filing,

00:29:27.325 --> 00:29:31.000
and PACER to look up records; state courts have their own.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:33.913
The court's confirmation is our proof of filing.

00:29:33.913 --> 00:29:36.850
Many courts serve registered lawyers automatically,

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:39.875
but anyone not registered, like someone without a lawyer,

00:29:39.875 --> 00:29:42.075
has to be served another way.

00:29:44.025 --> 00:29:47.938
First, before filing day, we check the rules: PDF format,

00:29:47.938 --> 00:29:52.725
page limits, file size, exhibit labels, fees and the cut-off time.

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:56.875
Next, we prepare exactly what the attorney approved,

00:29:56.875 --> 00:30:00.425
redact what the rules require and get final sign-off.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:05.000
Then we file early, because rejections take time to fix.

00:30:06.925 --> 00:30:11.175
After that, we save the filed copy and the confirmation together and send the

00:30:11.175 --> 00:30:13.325
confirmation to the attorney.

00:30:13.325 --> 00:30:18.038
Finally, we serve anyone outside the system and calendar the new deadlines the

00:30:18.038 --> 00:30:19.525
filing creates.

00:30:20.275 --> 00:30:21.350
Try this one.

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:24.013
It's 4 p.m. on the deadline day.

00:30:24.013 --> 00:30:28.463
The court's e-filing system rejects Elias's brief because an exhibit is over the

00:30:28.463 --> 00:30:29.950
file-size limit.

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:32.800
What do you do, in order, and who do you tell?

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:38.775
Miss the filing deadline for a claim, and the claim can be gone for good,

00:30:38.775 --> 00:30:40.525
however strong it was.

00:30:41.275 --> 00:30:44.450
That outer deadline is the statute of limitations,

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:48.488
and it changes with the type of claim and the state or country.

00:30:48.488 --> 00:30:53.225
A date that was right for last month's case can be completely wrong for this one.

00:30:54.025 --> 00:30:57.213
First, work it out and record it when the matter comes in,

00:30:57.213 --> 00:31:01.375
using the actual date of the event and the right jurisdiction's rule.

00:31:01.375 --> 00:31:06.050
Next, give it more warning time and reminders than a normal court date.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:11.763
Finally, if it's unclear which rule applies, ask the attorney.

00:31:11.763 --> 00:31:15.050
That's a legal judgment, not ours to make alone.

00:31:15.825 --> 00:31:18.013
Here's a situation to think about.

00:31:18.013 --> 00:31:22.538
A colleague copied the limitation deadline from a similar case last year.

00:31:22.538 --> 00:31:26.288
The new case is in a different state, with a government defendant.

00:31:26.288 --> 00:31:29.525
What's the risk, and who confirms the real date?

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:34.525
For a deposition, no reply doesn't mean yes.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:38.600
A deposition is a meeting with far more moving parts:

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:42.213
lawyers from both sides, the witness, a court reporter,

00:31:42.213 --> 00:31:44.200
sometimes an interpreter.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:49.250
Moving it later is expensive, so the work is all in the coordination up front.

00:31:50.050 --> 00:31:54.125
First, confirm everyone who has to be there before you lock the date.

00:31:54.125 --> 00:31:59.100
Next, book the court reporter and any interpreter the moment the date is set.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.350
They're usually the hardest to get.

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:07.575
Finally, send the formal notice promptly and keep track of who has actually

00:32:07.575 --> 00:32:08.725
confirmed.

00:32:09.475 --> 00:32:11.163
Take a second with this one.

00:32:11.163 --> 00:32:14.050
You've confirmed a date with the attorney and the witness,

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:17.150
but opposing counsel hasn't answered for days.

00:32:17.150 --> 00:32:19.125
Do you send the notice or wait?

00:32:19.125 --> 00:32:20.675
What do you actually do?

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:26.975
In a hearing, everyone has to be looking at exactly the same page at the same

00:32:26.975 --> 00:32:27.863
moment.

00:32:27.863 --> 00:32:30.825
That's what exhibits and Bates numbers are for.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:35.375
An exhibit is any document or object used as evidence,

00:32:35.375 --> 00:32:38.775
and each gets a label, like Exhibit 12 or Exhibit C.

00:32:38.775 --> 00:32:43.400
Bates numbering stamps a unique number on every page produced in discovery,

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:46.400
with a prefix and a padded number, like Thorne,

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:51.750
zero zero zero zero zero one, so any page can be found and there's a record of

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:53.975
exactly what was handed over.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:58.100
Binders usually come in several sets: the judge's,

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.225
the witness's, opposing counsel's and ours.

00:33:03.175 --> 00:33:06.700
First, we follow the court's and judge's rules for labels.

00:33:07.525 --> 00:33:11.100
Next, we keep a master exhibit list: number, description,

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:13.600
date, Bates range and status.

00:33:14.375 --> 00:33:18.175
Then we apply Bates numbers with software, never by hand,

00:33:18.175 --> 00:33:20.825
and never renumber a set that's been produced.

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:27.475
After that, we build every binder from the master list, with a tabbed index.

00:33:27.475 --> 00:33:32.425
Finally, we check every set page by page and keep a digital copy of each.

00:33:33.175 --> 00:33:38.538
Two days before a hearing, Elias adds three exhibits between Exhibits 7 and 8.

00:33:38.538 --> 00:33:41.213
Four binder sets are already printed.

00:33:41.213 --> 00:33:43.575
How do you handle the numbering and the binders,

00:33:43.575 --> 00:33:45.875
and what do you tell opposing counsel?

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.875
A trial isn't a busy week; it's a completely different way of working,

00:33:51.875 --> 00:33:54.400
so agree the rules before it starts.

00:33:55.150 --> 00:34:00.025
The attorney's day is squeezed and everything is urgent, so you're properly on call.

00:34:00.825 --> 00:34:05.413
First, before trial starts, ask exactly what support the attorney will need:

00:34:05.413 --> 00:34:07.975
documents, research, exhibits.

00:34:07.975 --> 00:34:12.325
Next, organize every document, contact and practical detail,

00:34:12.325 --> 00:34:16.550
like the courthouse address and parking, so you can find it instantly.

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:21.350
Finally, agree how you'll communicate during trial:

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:24.125
which channel, and how fast you'll respond.

00:34:24.875 --> 00:34:26.575
Take a second with this one.

00:34:26.575 --> 00:34:32.250
On day two of a trial, Elias texts at 7 a.m. asking for an exhibit that isn't in the

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:33.613
trial binder.

00:34:33.613 --> 00:34:36.875
What should have been done before trial, and what do you do now?

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:41.888
Section 4 of 4: Travel Management.

00:34:41.888 --> 00:34:46.600
Eleven lessons on planning travel end-to-end, from itineraries and visas to

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:49.825
contingency plans and the post-trip debrief.

00:34:51.725 --> 00:34:55.625
Plan the trip backward, starting from what has to go right when the executive

00:34:55.625 --> 00:34:56.750
arrives.

00:34:57.550 --> 00:35:01.912
Most people plan forward: flight, then hotel, then 'we'll sort out the car.'

00:35:01.912 --> 00:35:03.925
Planning backward flips that.

00:35:05.875 --> 00:35:09.062
First, ask what Elias must be ready for, and when.

00:35:09.062 --> 00:35:12.425
Then work back through the car, the hotel and the flights.

00:35:13.250 --> 00:35:18.600
Next, for international trips, check the visa and document rules well ahead of time.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.838
Finally, leave real room between connecting flights,

00:35:23.838 --> 00:35:28.350
especially when he lands internationally and has to clear customs again.

00:35:29.125 --> 00:35:31.312
Here's a situation to think about.

00:35:31.312 --> 00:35:35.675
Elias's inbox has seven separate confirmation emails for one trip.

00:35:35.675 --> 00:35:40.600
He asks, 'What time is my car tomorrow?' What should you have sent him instead?

00:35:42.525 --> 00:35:47.250
Check the entry rules for this trip today; don't rely on what was true last time.

00:35:48.025 --> 00:35:53.188
A trip can be ruined at the airport by a passport that's valid but not valid enough,

00:35:53.188 --> 00:35:56.350
or a visa that takes weeks longer than expected.

00:35:58.325 --> 00:36:01.388
First, check how long the passport is still valid.

00:36:01.388 --> 00:36:04.925
Many countries want six months left after the trip ends.

00:36:06.875 --> 00:36:08.975
Then, start any visa early.

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:15.300
After that, sort supporting papers like invitation letters early enough to fix

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:16.450
problems.

00:36:16.450 --> 00:36:21.150
Finally, write down what was needed, so the next trip starts with facts.

00:36:21.925 --> 00:36:23.438
Work through this one.

00:36:23.438 --> 00:36:26.925
Elias flies to Singapore for a deposition in five weeks,

00:36:26.925 --> 00:36:29.750
and his passport expires in four months.

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:33.200
What do you check, what do you do today and what do you tell him?

00:36:35.125 --> 00:36:38.925
Going abroad means planning for health, money and local customs,

00:36:38.925 --> 00:36:41.075
not just flights and hotels.

00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:45.575
None of it is hard, but all of it needs doing before he leaves.

00:36:47.525 --> 00:36:50.663
First, check health and safety for the destination:

00:36:50.663 --> 00:36:55.125
vaccinations, safety advisories and local emergency numbers.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:57.775
Next, sort out how he'll pay.

00:36:57.775 --> 00:37:00.475
Will his card work there, and does he need cash?

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:05.838
Then, learn the local business customs, how meetings run and how people greet each

00:37:05.838 --> 00:37:08.225
other, so he doesn't get caught out.

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:14.650
After that, look at the government travel advisory again close to departure.

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:19.375
Finally, give international trips more planning time than domestic ones.

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:22.263
Here's a situation to work through.

00:37:22.263 --> 00:37:24.863
You booked Elias's trip three weeks ago.

00:37:24.863 --> 00:37:29.375
Two days before he leaves, the government issues an advisory about protests near his

00:37:29.375 --> 00:37:30.325
hotel.

00:37:30.325 --> 00:37:31.425
What do you do?

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:36.825
In a trip with several stops, look for the tightest connection,

00:37:36.825 --> 00:37:38.750
because that's where it will break.

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:42.750
With one flight there and back, a delay is annoying.

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:47.500
With four legs, a delay on the first can knock over every connection after it.

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:52.175
So first, lay out every leg together, not one at a time.

00:37:52.175 --> 00:37:56.025
Next, find the tightest connection and give it extra time.

00:37:57.950 --> 00:38:03.150
Then, make sure each car pickup and hotel check-in matches the real arrival times.

00:38:05.075 --> 00:38:07.775
After that, put the whole trip on one page.

00:38:07.775 --> 00:38:12.975
Finally, check it all again just before departure, in case any times have shifted.

00:38:13.725 --> 00:38:18.075
Pause the video and look at this trip: New York to Atlanta with a 50-minute

00:38:18.075 --> 00:38:21.450
connection, on to Dallas for two days of trial prep,

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:24.138
then Dallas to Phoenix for a mediation.

00:38:24.138 --> 00:38:27.000
Where's the weak point, and what would you change?

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:32.725
'We'll figure out a car when we land' is how good trips go wrong.

00:38:33.475 --> 00:38:37.850
The car gets treated as an afterthought, until the executive is standing at the curb

00:38:37.850 --> 00:38:39.688
at night with no ride.

00:38:39.688 --> 00:38:42.350
Plan the ride as carefully as the flight.

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:48.075
First, confirm the exact pickup time, the exact spot and a phone number for the

00:38:48.075 --> 00:38:49.125
driver.

00:38:51.075 --> 00:38:54.425
Then, check who's traveling: children need car seats,

00:38:54.425 --> 00:38:56.950
and some people need accessible vehicles.

00:38:58.875 --> 00:39:02.550
After that, for important trips, have a backup ride ready.

00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:06.525
Finally, reconfirm the booking a day or so before travel.

00:39:07.275 --> 00:39:09.312
Pause the video and try this.

00:39:09.312 --> 00:39:14.362
Elias and his two young children land in Orlando at 9:40 PM.

00:39:14.362 --> 00:39:17.025
Write the ride confirmation you'd send him.

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:23.913
Adding the loyalty number to every booking is a small habit that pays off on every

00:39:23.913 --> 00:39:25.100
single trip.

00:39:25.875 --> 00:39:29.738
Those numbers bring upgrades, priority service and status.

00:39:29.738 --> 00:39:33.150
Leaving one off is money and comfort left on the table.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:39.575
First, keep all the membership numbers with Elias's other preferences.

00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:43.700
Next, add them to every booking as a normal step.

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:50.100
Then, apply his seat, route and hotel preferences at the same time.

00:39:52.025 --> 00:39:53.963
After that, put it on a checklist.

00:39:53.963 --> 00:39:58.400
Finally, check the details now and then, because status levels change.

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:01.438
Here's a situation to think about.

00:40:01.438 --> 00:40:05.825
A substitute assistant books Elias a middle seat with a connecting flight,

00:40:05.825 --> 00:40:07.738
not knowing his preferences.

00:40:07.738 --> 00:40:10.225
What in your process would have prevented this?

00:40:12.125 --> 00:40:16.175
The backup plan should exist before the trip, not be invented in the middle of a

00:40:16.175 --> 00:40:17.275
crisis.

00:40:18.025 --> 00:40:22.075
When a flight is cancelled, the assistant who planned ahead is already making the

00:40:22.075 --> 00:40:25.100
call while everyone else is still searching.

00:40:25.875 --> 00:40:30.525
First, before you finalize the trip, list the realistic things that could go wrong.

00:40:30.525 --> 00:40:34.325
Next, find the backup for each in advance: the next flight,

00:40:34.325 --> 00:40:36.725
another route, a local contact.

00:40:38.675 --> 00:40:42.975
Then, keep those backups with the itinerary, where you can find them fast.

00:40:44.925 --> 00:40:48.725
After that, if something happens, use the backup straight away.

00:40:48.725 --> 00:40:53.300
It's the same idea as having a backup vendor: it's ready before you need it.

00:40:54.050 --> 00:40:56.513
Pause the video and work this one out.

00:40:56.513 --> 00:41:01.738
Elias flies Chicago to Denver, then on to Boise for a 10 AM hearing.

00:41:01.738 --> 00:41:05.475
What are the two most likely problems, and what's the backup for each?

00:41:07.475 --> 00:41:10.250
Know the one time the trip absolutely has to hit,

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:12.775
and the backup, before the plane leaves.

00:41:13.525 --> 00:41:17.650
On a court appearance or a closing, a cancelled flight can wreck the whole reason

00:41:17.650 --> 00:41:18.750
for going.

00:41:19.550 --> 00:41:22.400
First, identify the deadline the travel must meet,

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:24.900
like the hearing time, before you book.

00:41:24.900 --> 00:41:28.275
Next, know the backups ahead of time: a later flight,

00:41:28.275 --> 00:41:31.200
a different airport or a car for the last stretch.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:37.500
Finally, when something goes wrong, send the executive one clear message with the

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:40.875
problem and your plan, not a stream of updates.

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:44.350
A storm grounds flights out of Chicago.

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:49.813
Elias is stuck overnight, and his 9 a.m. client meeting in New York is at risk.

00:41:49.813 --> 00:41:53.750
What options do you prepare, and what do you tell the client, and when?

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:59.875
On the road, receipts end up in pockets, cars and hotel bins,

00:41:59.875 --> 00:42:05.475
and rebuilding a trip's expenses a week later from memory is slow and usually wrong.

00:42:06.275 --> 00:42:10.425
First, capture each receipt immediately, even just a photo.

00:42:10.425 --> 00:42:14.900
Next, note which client or matter it belongs to while you still remember.

00:42:16.825 --> 00:42:21.125
Then, handle it the same way as normal expenses, just faster.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:27.725
After that, spend two minutes each evening checking nothing from the day is missing.

00:42:27.725 --> 00:42:32.000
Finally, submit everything within a set time after getting back.

00:42:32.775 --> 00:42:35.050
Here's a situation to work through.

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:38.988
Elias returns from a four-day trip with a crumpled pile of receipts:

00:42:38.988 --> 00:42:44.913
two client dinners, three taxis, a hotel, a personal gift and a coffee with no note.

00:42:44.913 --> 00:42:46.350
How do you sort them?

00:42:48.275 --> 00:42:52.075
A checklist that lives only in your head isn't really a checklist.

00:42:52.850 --> 00:42:56.600
Every time you rely on memory, you forget something different.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:43:00.713
Write it down once, use it every trip and improve it a little each time,

00:43:00.713 --> 00:43:02.475
just like the Home Binder.

00:43:04.425 --> 00:43:07.400
First, give passport and visa their own section,

00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:09.925
so they never get lost among the bookings.

00:43:10.750 --> 00:43:14.225
Next, add health and safety prep for the destination.

00:43:16.175 --> 00:43:19.250
Then, include a check that loyalty numbers were added,

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:21.525
and a contact for emergencies.

00:43:21.525 --> 00:43:24.300
Finally, keep it somewhere you'll find it.

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:27.263
Pause the video and try this.

00:43:27.263 --> 00:43:31.675
Elias's last trip had three small misses: no power adapter,

00:43:31.675 --> 00:43:35.838
a missing loyalty number and no emergency contact for the hotel.

00:43:35.838 --> 00:43:38.125
Turn those into checklist items.

00:43:40.025 --> 00:43:42.450
The trip isn't over when the traveler gets home;

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:45.088
the last steps make the next trip better.

00:43:45.088 --> 00:43:48.050
After a trip, three things usually get skipped:

00:43:48.050 --> 00:43:52.075
the expenses, the thank-yous and writing down what went wrong.

00:43:52.875 --> 00:43:56.175
First, reconcile the expenses soon after return.

00:43:56.175 --> 00:43:59.225
Next, send any thank-you notes or follow-ups.

00:44:01.175 --> 00:44:05.250
Then, write a short note on what worked and what didn't while it's fresh.

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:09.850
That last one matters most, because otherwise the same problem happens again next

00:44:09.850 --> 00:44:10.800
time.

00:44:12.725 --> 00:44:17.000
After that, feed that note into the travel preferences or checklist.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:21.625
It's the same improvement habit you'll use for bigger projects on Day 6.

00:44:22.375 --> 00:44:24.538
Pause the video and try this.

00:44:24.538 --> 00:44:27.925
Elias's Denver hotel had no quiet place to work,

00:44:27.925 --> 00:44:30.475
and his return connection was too tight.

00:44:30.475 --> 00:44:34.500
Write the debrief note, and say where each lesson gets recorded.

00:44:36.425 --> 00:44:38.638
Time to practice what you just learned.

00:44:38.638 --> 00:44:41.875
In the Calendar Conflict Resolver and Travel Planning lab,

00:44:41.875 --> 00:44:45.075
you'll take a full attorney's week of overlapping commitments,

00:44:45.075 --> 00:44:48.025
resolve it and turn it into a BLUF briefing.

00:44:48.025 --> 00:44:52.500
Then you'll plan the attorney's business trip to a Chicago arbitration end-to-end:

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:55.925
flights, hotel, ground transport and stops.

00:44:55.925 --> 00:44:57.425
It's your turn now.

